system of a priori unknown dynamics show that the presence of haptic feedback signiﬁcantly helps the operator in a visually guided tracking task [5]. Similar results have been identiﬁed in teleoperation tasks in the ﬁeld of robotics. In particular, Hannaford and Wood [6] compared manipulation performance under three conditions: direct manual control, and remote telerobotic control with and without force feedback. In teleoperation conditions, it was found that the presence of force feedback increased operator performance signiﬁcantly (30% reduction in operation time, 60% decrease in positioning error, 7-fold reduction in exerted force). Similarly, Howe and Kontarinis [7] compared operator performance in a two degreeof-freedom peg-insertion task with and without force feedback. Their results showed that time-to-completion was reduced by a factor of two with force feedback. In the case of vehicle steering, Bertollini and Hogan [8] showed on a dynamic driving simulator that the drivers’ preference for steering wheel torque feedback is dependant on vehicle speed, in agreement with previous ﬁndings on real vehicles [9] showing that drivers prefer steering effort to increase with vehicle speed.
Drivers steer their vehicle by means of a steering system composed typically of a wheel, steering column, Cardan joint, rack-and-pinion, and tierods steering arms connected to the wheels’ kingpins. The torque appearing on the steering wheel is the resultant of the road contact forces applied to the tyres, transmitted by the steering system. Additional power steering systems can modify this resulting torque for enhanced driver comfort using either hydraulic or electric technology. Future ‘steer-by-wire’ systems will allow the mechanical decoupling of the steering wheel from the driving wheels, and the generation of any type of steering force feedback.
However, whatever the design of the system, the static steering force feedback is approximately proportional to the steering angle at a given vehicle speed and road adherence, and within limited vehicle rates of turn. In fact, the force appearing at the road–tyre interface is mainly proportional to the vehicle lateral acceleration, and this force is transmitted to the steering wheel with minimal distortion at low steering angles [10]. Therefore, the steering wheel force feedback is carrying relevant information regarding the instantaneous dynamics of the driven vehicle. This information – being physically related to vehicle speed and trajectory curvature – could be used by drivers to reinforce the visual information used to steer their vehicle. For instance, driving experiments on a real car and driving simulator have shown that perceived lateral acceleration is a cue actually used by drivers to adjust their speed in curves [11]. Yet, no experiments were carried out to show whether steering wheel force feedback is actually used by drivers to adjust their internal model of the vehicle dynamics. Alternatively, the role of steering force feedback could also be to help drivers control their steering wheel position in a robust way. Indeed, experiments on the manual control of dynamic systems have shown that underlying neuromuscular mechanisms must somehow integrate a representation of the mechanical compliance (i.e. force in relation to displacement) of both the corresponding limbs and the control actuators [12]. Another study has demonstrated that compensatory movements in response to changes in visuomotor coupling were not consciously detected, and that subjects adapted their motor control strategy unconsciously within certain limits [13].
To test these two hypotheses, we designed a new driving experiment on the RENAULT dynamic driving simulator, allowing us to render different types of steering force feedback in the same driving task. Results show a very wide range of adaptation of the drivers to the various steering wheel dynamics tested. We propose a new driver model to reproduce this effect, and to analyse whether this adaptation is taking place at a haptic level or at a different level related to the internal model of car dynamics.

