2. Experimentation

2.1 Driving simulator
The RENAULT dynamic driving simulator used in this experiment is based on an actual Clio II cockpit with fully operational commands (steering wheel, pedals, gearshift, etc.) and animated dashboards dials (ﬁgure 1). An electric force feedback system is coupled to the steering wheel to render the steering torque up to 8 Nm in the conﬁguration used in the experiments, which is computed by the real-time advanced vehicle dynamics simulation software MADA developed by RENAULT. This model is a high-end simulation tool used by vehicle engineers for the design of drivetrains and vehicle systems; its multi-body structure computes the dynamics of the chassis, wheels, engine and steering column, based on numerical data describing the behavior of the tyres, wind resistance, and brakes. A REXROTHHYDRAUDYNE (Boxtel, The Netherlands) 1000 kg six-axes electric motion platform is used to produce cockpit accelerations of up to 0.5 g within a limited motion envelope, therefore rendering a part of the car dynamics in normal driving situations [14]. The driver is fully immersed in a virtual scene of the road, composed of a 150◦ wide frontal image and three back-projection screens for the rear-view mirrors. Real-time digital images are computed by a SGI ONYX2 workstation and by PC-based image generators. Six projectors display the road scene, three for the front view and three for the rear-view mirrors. A complete description of the simulator conﬁguration can be found in Reymond and Kemeny [15].

