2.2 Protocol
Five participants from the RENAULT staff completed the experiment (mean age 29, average driving mileage 15.000 km/year). Among them, one participant (S3) was quite experienced in driving this simulator; the others had never driven on a simulator before. Drivers with no simulator experience were chosen deliberately to avoid the ‘adaptability’ of experimented drivers to simulator artifacts, which could mask the effect considered in this experiment.
Drivers were placed on a realistic virtual reproduction of one of RENAULT’s proving grounds (ﬁgure 2) and drove ﬁve laps in a row for each experiment. Instructions were given to drive at a constant speed (60 km/h) and to keep in the middle of the lane. Steering force feedback modiﬁcations were activated by the simulator in a straight part of the road (see circle in ﬁgure 2), so that the drivers would not be aware of it. During the simulation, several data were recorded at 20 Hz: steering wheel angle, absolute vehicle position, lateral position, vehicle speed, vehicle acceleration, and local road curvature. Only the data recorded in the ﬁrst two curves after the modiﬁcation of steering feedback are presented in this study. Modiﬁcations of the steering force feedback used in the experiment were chosen to be very clearly distinguishable in a non-driving control situation.

2.2.1 Experiment 1: Modiﬁcation of steering wheel torque with two linear laws. During the ﬁrst three laps, a steering feedback law K1 was used in the simulator and was then changed to K2 before the fourth lap. These two feedback laws are linear relative to steering angle, and may be represented by a stiffness coefﬁcient 1.5 Nm/rad for K1 and 2.5 Nm/rad for K2 (ﬁgure 3).

