2.3 Analysis
For each driver and for each of the different recorded variables (steering wheel angle, lateral deviation, and lateral acceleration), a root mean square (RMS) difference, deﬁned by RMS(x, y) = ni=1(xi − yi)2/n, was computed between conﬁgurations K1 and K2, and between K1 and K3. This RMS difference expresses the increase or decrease of steering variability, and is therefore chosen as an indicator of driver adaptation to a modiﬁcation of the simulated vehicle characteristics. A reference value is computed separately for K1 by comparing the RMS difference between the ﬁrst and second laps before the steering modiﬁcation occurs. This reference value expresses the basic variability of vehicle steering for each driver, and is expressed for each recorded variable.

