The RMS obtained in conﬁgurations K2 and K3 are compared to the reference RMS K1. The differences obtained are expressed in m/s2 for lateral acceleration, in meters for lateral deviation, and in degrees for steering angle (table 1). When this difference is negative or below a certain threshold, the steering variability has decreased, thus indicating an adaptation of steering strategy to the new steering force conﬁguration. A threshold is chosen for each variable to identify the modiﬁcation of the driver’s strategy. For the lateral acceleration, this threshold value is 0.05 m/s2; corresponding to a vestibular detection psychophysical threshold [16]. For lateral deviation, the threshold value is 0.125 m, corresponding to an error of 5% inside a 2.5 m wide lane. For steering angle, the threshold value is 3◦, corresponding to an error of 5% relative to the 60◦ steering angle in the sharpest turn (R = 40 m). When the difference is positive and above these thresholds, the steering variability has increased, and the steering performance has degraded in response to the change of steering conﬁguration.
The variables recorded during these experimentations have different meanings relative to the steering control task. The main performance criterion is the lateral deviation, since driving instructions were to stay in the middle of the lane. However, lateral acceleration and steering angle have also been analysed to help characterize the modiﬁcations in steering control strategy.

2.4 Results
The RMS results of lateral acceleration (table 1) show that the drivers S1 (ﬁgure 4) and S4 have modiﬁed their vehicle control behavior when the steering force feedback was modiﬁed to K2, and that for driver S2 the difference was below the 5% threshold. The result for driver S5 in this case is probably accidental, considering his correct adaptation with the K3 conﬁguration. With the steering force feedback K3, drivers S2, S4, and S5 show a behavior also comparable to the steering force feedback K1.
The RMS results for lateral deviation (table 1) show that all ﬁve drivers have changed their vehicle control behavior by maintaining their steering control performance with the force feedback modiﬁcation K2. With the steering force feedback K3, drivers S2, S4, and S5 show the same change of their vehicle control behavior, since the RMS difference in lateral deviation was below the 0.1 m threshold.
The RMS results for the steering angle (Table 1) show that the drivers S1, S2, S3, and S4 have changed their steering behavior to the force feedback K2. The result for driver S5 in this case is again probably accidental, considering his correct adaptation with the K3 conﬁguration and the similar observation for the lateral acceleration. With the law K3, a negative difference was observed for the drivers S2, S3, and S4, and a positive difference was observed for the driver S1 but below the threshold.
In total, adaptation of steering behavior has occurred for all of the ﬁve drivers in both conﬁgurations, as indicated by a signiﬁcant decrease in steering variability shown by at least one experimental variable.

2.4.1 Control experiment. For this experiment where particular steering wheel torque feedback was used (K4 = 0 and K5 = −K1), the observation is that no driver could drive properly, nor adapt his vehicle control strategy. All drivers stopped the experiment voluntarily because they could not control correctly the position on their vehicle in the middle of the lane.

