3. Model
Driver steering models are generally ‘preview’ models, based on the observation that drivers control the anticipated lateral position of their vehicle on the road at a look-ahead distance equivalent to a ﬁxed preview time [17, 18]. In such models, visual information available to the driver [3] is generally considered sufﬁcient to estimate accurately this preview position on the road. The desired steering angle has to be transformed into an actual steering wheel position, a task that requires haptic interaction with a steering system. Corresponding models for the motor control of complex dynamic systems are generally based on the ‘crossover’ model, based on the observation that human operators adapt their manual control in such a way that the resulting coupled system has a crossover frequency ωc equal to that of the stand-alone dynamic system [19].
By combining these two classical models, we propose a two-level driver model representing the interaction between the driver vehicle steering strategy and the haptic control mechanism executing it (ﬁgure 5). This model, implemented in a simple form, comprises only two main driver parameters, which represent the internal models of the vehicle angular dynamics, and of the steering force feedback characteristics. McAdam [20] proposed a similar model scheme with two main driver parameters, and explained that the characterization of driver behavior cannot be separated into a purely mechanical and a purely human component, but is rather to

