More realistic models for the vehicle dynamics may be used though, while still assuming that the drivers’ internal model of their vehicle is based on this simpler linear model, as described further. For instance, a linear ‘bicycle model’ including side-slip angle on the basis of ref. [21] was tested later on: it produced only minor differences in driving performance on the scenarios considered in our experiment, and mainly during fast steering phases as expected. Developing a driver model that would account for these second-order effects is out of the present scope, but could be considered nonetheless within the framework of our general model.

3.2 Preview steering model
The steering model part represents the driver steering response α to control the vehicle lateral
deviation y, knowing the road geometry ahead.
As a direct application of McAdam’s preview model, the driver is assumed to look ahead
on the road at a certain distance deﬁned by a ﬁxed preview time Tp, and to steer the vehicle in order to bring the estimated future vehicle path in line with the future desired path using a
simple negative feedback mechanism. The estimation of the future lateral position of the vehicle y(t + Tp) is assumed to be
the result of a second-order extrapolation from its current position y(t): y(t + Tp) = y(t) + Tpy˙(t) + (Tp2/2)y¨(t) − εr(Tp). The correction term εr(Tp) for y accounts for the curvature of the road centerline and is geometrically deﬁned by εr(Tp) = Rr[1 − cos(V Tp/Rr)]; for usual values of Tp of few seconds, a simple Taylor expansion yields εr(Tp) = (V Tp)2/2Rr. The performance of this preview estimation mechanism can be shown experimentally to be
quite good even when only optic ﬂow cues are available [22]. The difference between y(t + Tp) and the desired future lateral position y0(t + Tp) is used
as a feedback term to deﬁne a corrective steering angle α = Ke−Ts(y0(t + Tp) − y(t + Tp)). The time delay expressed in Laplace notation e−Ts represents the amount of time necessary for the driver to process the visual information and estimate y(t + Tp). The transfer function K = αSW/y(t + Tp) represents the lateral deviation of the vehicle from its initial straight course, measured after a preview time Tp with constant steer angle. This deviation is geometrically identical to the above expression of εr(Tp), using a radius of turn RV = Lk/α deﬁned by the driver steering angle. From this, K is derived as K = 2Lkˆ/V 2Tp2, where the parameter kˆ represents the driver internal model of the angular dynamics of the vehicle, which is equal to
