3.5 Discussion
By assuming an ideal adaptation, the driver model is able to reproduce the steering performance observed in the simulator experiment with different steering conﬁgurations. The model also shows that adaptation can occur for a range of vehicle dynamics parameters, but that accurate steering is impossible in extreme situations, e.g. with a very stiff steering wheel or with a total absence of force feedback.
The model also shows that a change of one vehicle physical parameter (kV or ZV) cannot be compensated for by adapting another internal driver parameter. Therefore, a modiﬁcation in steering wheel force feedback is not likely to be interpreted by drivers as a change of the vehicle dynamics, and is to be compensated for by an adaptation of their internal model of the steering wheel compliance. This suggests that steering force feedback is an input only used by drivers in the manual control of the steering wheel, even though it carries information tightly coupled to the vehicle dynamics.
However, the actual preference of standard drivers may favor a narrower range of conﬁgurations for everyday driving than indicated by the model adaptation range. In particular, expert or racing drivers may have quite different requirements, as being able to fully exploit haptic information for better driving performance. Besides, the preferred increase in steering stiffness with vehicle speed mentioned by Green et al. [9] is not addressed in the present model; this effect could be related to a requirement of increased steering accuracy, represented in the model by the ωc crossover frequency parameter.
Further developments of the proposed model could include the role of steering force feedback as a cognitive cue, in particular indicating sudden drops of road adherence calling for immediate driver action. Similarly, a more realistic vehicle dynamics model could be included in the model to represent the effects of e.g. side-slip, load transfer, oversteer/understeer on the steering performance of the car. However, under stable handling conditions, the same driver model should apply as well, based on the assumption that the ‘internal model’ parameters kˆV and Zˆ V correspond to estimations of the linearized steering behavior of the car.

4. Conclusion
The adaptation to steering force feedback modiﬁcations observed in our experiment suggests that drivers act as controllers of a steering wheel position instead of a steering torque. Indeed, in order to achieve the same driving performance in terms of steering accuracy in response to a force feedback modiﬁcation, the driver must apply a different steering torque while performing the same movements.
Other results could be related to our ﬁndings. In a purely haptic task, in which operators are asked to reproduce the direction of a force without visual information [25], it can be shown that the haptic force exerted is related to a displacement of the arm’s equilibrium point, independently of the mechanical impedance characteristics of the manipulated system. In this experiment, when the initially isotropic impedance of the mechanical system becomes anisotropic, the direction of the force to be reproduced by subjects is modiﬁed, because they reproduce a displacement instead. Moreover, in visuo-motor tasks, subject adaptation has also been shown to happen unconsciously [13], although subjects would notice the change in the manipulated system when visual feedback is not available. It is interesting to note that, in our experiment, the modiﬁcation of the wheel impedance also resulted in a reproduction of the angular position of the steering wheel by drivers, without their being aware of the modiﬁcation itself.

