This experiment and driver model therefore contradicts the hypothesis whereby haptic steering information could be used by standard drivers as a cue reﬂecting the dynamics of their vehicle. The driver’s internal model of the vehicle dynamics is more likely built separately from other visual and vestibular information. The build-up mechanism of such an internal model from multi-sensory information should be further investigated, using high-performance dynamic simulators which were not available at the time of our experiment. Future developments of intelligent steering assistance (i.e. electric power steering and ‘steer-by-wire’systems) may beneﬁt from this driver behavior study methodology and model, by designing steering force feedback strategies that are best adapted to the visuo-motor characteristics of the drivers.

